{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 820,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sure black holes are awesome, but  how about black holes being"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4200,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "captured by the screaming vortex of a quasar, where"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7040,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they merge and grow like some monstrous version\nof a solar system. This insane hypothesis"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12860,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is getting closer to reality, at least according to the papers in today’s Space Time Journal club."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24000,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In September 2015 the laser interferometer\ngravitational wave observatory - LIGO - detected"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30060,
    "dDurationMs": 5769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its first gravitational wave from the merger\nof two black holes. That was stunning enough,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35829,
    "dDurationMs": 4421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the real promise lay ahead. Every time\nwe learn to observe the universe in a new"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40250,
    "dDurationMs": 6310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way we discover new things. When we figured\nout how to see in radio waves quasars and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46560,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supernova remnants lit up the sky, When we\nlearned to see in neutrinos the core of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52190,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sun became visible to us. But now that our\nvision extends to gravitational waves, what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57780,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "else might we discover?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59970,
    "dDurationMs": 5019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The black hole mergers themselves were not\nso surprising. Einstein’s general relativity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64989,
    "dDurationMs": 6621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "predicted gravitational waves and astrophysics\npredicted black hole mergers. When two very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71610,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "massive stars are in binary orbit with each\nother, they may end their lives to leave a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76570,
    "dDurationMs": 6110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pair of binary black holes. And in very dense\nenvironments like the cores of galaxies, lone"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82680,
    "dDurationMs": 6110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes may find each other and form a\nbinary pair. However the pair forms, it can’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88790,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "last forever. As they circle each other, black\nholes whip the fabric of space into expanding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93390,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ripples - gravitational waves - which saps\norbital energy from the system. The black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99150,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes spiral closer and closer together. In\nthe last instant they coalesce into a single"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104030,
    "dDurationMs": 5019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole, and the powerful gravitational\nwaves produced in the last fraction of a second"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109049,
    "dDurationMs": 5281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are what LIGO detects - sometimes from over\na billion light years away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114330,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We expected to see black hole mergers, but\nthere was some striking surprises. For one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120350,
    "dDurationMs": 4189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing, many of the merging black holes were\ntoo massive to have been formed by the collapse"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124539,
    "dDurationMs": 4791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of stellar cores. That is if our understanding\nof stellar evolution is half as good as we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129330,
    "dDurationMs": 7070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "think it is. This led astrophysicists to think\nof new ways to produce black hole mergers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136400,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s the most awesome possibility: what\nif black hole mergers actually occur in orbit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141690,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around supermassive black holes, embedded\ndeep in the whirlpools of searing gas that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146760,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surround some of these monsters? Well today on Space Time Journal Club we’ll be looking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151340,
    "dDurationMs": 8680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at a pair of 2019 papers that talk about this\npossibility. We have Yang Yang Imre Bartos et al., which predicts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160020,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the properties of black holes that merge this\nway, and Barry McKernan Saavik Ford et al."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165380,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which proposes a way for us to actually test this hypothesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170780,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The argument goes like this: we know that\nthe center of almost every galaxy contains"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176330,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a supermassive black hole of millions to billions\nof times the mass of the Sun. Recently we’ve"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182690,
    "dDurationMs": 5850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also learned that the galactic center likely\nalso contains a swarm of perhaps tens of thousands"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188540,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of stellar-mass black holes. These are the\nremnants of dead stars, typically a few to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193650,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a few tens times the mass of the Sun. They\nrained down on the galactic center over billions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199480,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of years as massive stars formed and died\nin the surrounding galactic core. This has"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205100,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been a theoretical prediction for some time,\nbut we’ve recently found evidence of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209450,
    "dDurationMs": 4710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Milky Way’s black hole swarm - and yeah,\nwe covered that in a previous episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214160,
    "dDurationMs": 7030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so, a black hole swarm surrounding a supermassive\nblack hole sounds like a recipe for black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221190,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole collisions. Actually not so much - black\nholes are so compact that they never collide"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226970,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outright - they need to merge by first forming\na binary pair and then falling together. Now"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232350,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "binary black hole pairs surely do exist in\nthe dense galactic center, but they may have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237730,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trouble merging in such a dense environment.\nRegular glancing encounters with other stars"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243870,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or black holes can tear binary pairs apart\nbefore they can spiral together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248470,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s a way to massively accelerate\nthe mergers of these black holes: all you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253680,
    "dDurationMs": 5850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need is a little quasar. For the most part\nthe supermassive black holes at galactic centers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259530,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are, well, black. But occasionally gas from\nthe surrounding galaxy will find its way into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264840,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the galactic center and form an incandescent\nvortex - an accretion disk - as it plummets"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270540,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the insane gravitational field of the\ncentral monster. In the case of the largest,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275850,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most well-fed black holes this results in\nthe quasar phenomenon, and their accretion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280690,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disks glow bright enough to be visible from\nthe other side of the universe. More generally,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285730,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these feeding supermassive black holes are\ncalled active galactic nuclei."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290910,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is how supermassive black holes can grow\nto such enormous sizes, but what does the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295460,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "presence of an accretion disk mean for the\nswarm of stellar mass black holes? The orbits"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301090,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of those black holes are mostly random, so\nthe swarm forms a spheroid a few light years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306400,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "across. The accretion disk is quite a bit\nsmaller than the full swarm, but there should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310980,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still be plenty of black holes orbiting in\nthe region. These will punch right through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316290,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the accretion disk twice every orbit. On each\npass a streamer of gas is dragged out of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322060,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disk, tugged by the black hole’s gravitational\nfield. Momentum is transferred from black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328100,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole to gas, slowing the black hole down a\nbit and causing its orbit to decay - much"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333000,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like how a satellite’s orbit will decay if it’s\ntoo close to Earth’s atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337640,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eventually these disk-crossing black holes\nshould be swept into the accretion disk. There"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343250,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they gorge on the gas of the disk and grow\nin mass much, much faster than they could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347990,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in almost anywhere else in the galaxy. So\nnow we have one way for these black holes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352310,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to get big. But in order to be detected by\nLIGO, they also need to merge with each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358040,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are two ways this can happen: If a binary\nblack hole pair gets captured by the disk,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363460,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the surrounding gas saps their orbital energy\nmuch more quickly than by gravitational radiation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368530,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alone. This means they can spiral together\nbefore being ripped apart by a glancing blow"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373630,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with another object."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375970,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And accretion disks also allow lone black\nholes to find each other. This is really cool,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382440,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because the process is similar to how planets\nform. When a massive object is embedded in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388310,
    "dDurationMs": 6079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a rotating disk, it will exert a gravitational\ntug on the surrounding particles. Depending"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394389,
    "dDurationMs": 5171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the local properties of the disk, that\ncan cause the object to either gain or lose"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "angular momentum. If it gains angular momentum\nthe size of its orbit increases, so it moves"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405320,
    "dDurationMs": 7980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "further out in the disk - or “migrates outward”.\nIf it loses angular moment it migrates inwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413300,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the local properties of a disk will change\nwith the distance from the center. In some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417940,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regions you get inward migration and in some\nregions outward migration. The boundaries"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423400,
    "dDurationMs": 6290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between these regions are called migration\ntraps - no migration occurs there. So an embedded"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429690,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "object will wander inwards or outwards until\nthey find one of these traps, and they’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434790,
    "dDurationMs": 2730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remain stuck there for some time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437520,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In infant planetary systems, a disk of gas\nand dust surrounds the newly-formed star - a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443180,
    "dDurationMs": 6580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "protoplanetary disk. Lumps of coagulated ice\nand dust migrate to these traps, where they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can find each other and build into planets.\nIn the case of accretion disks, the “planets”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455280,
    "dDurationMs": 5991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are black holes - captured single black holes\nend up in the same migration trap, eventually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461271,
    "dDurationMs": 6639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "finding each other and forming binary pairs, which then quickly merge. This mechanism helps lone black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467910,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes find each other, so it should massively\nboost the number mergers. And boosting the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473290,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mergers also helps black holes to grow in\nmass more quickly. Multiple black holes can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478160,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "end up in the same migration trap and merge\none at a time, ultimately reaching enormous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483010,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sizes. This is one of the calculations of\nYang and collaborators: they figure that black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488920,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes merging in this way should have much\nhigher masses than via the “traditional”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493450,
    "dDurationMs": 5710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "empty-space mergers - with 50-solar-mass mergers\nbeing relatively common. Another paper by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499160,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Jillian Bellovary and co. predicts that behemoth\n“intermediate mass” black holes can form,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504650,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with 1000s of times the mass of the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507330,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So all of this sounds exciting and fun - and\nit may explain why so many surprisingly massive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512740,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole mergers are observed. And if we\nspot more and more high-mass mergers that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518290,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will be further evidence in favour of the\nhypothesis. Yang and co. also predict a particular"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524180,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distribution of black hole spins - again to\nbe tested with more LIGO observations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530360,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what about a more direct test? That’s where\nthe paper by McKernan and collaborators comes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536640,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in. If black holes merge in empty space then\nthe event should invisible - it should emit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no electromagnetic radiation. But it’s different\nin an accretion disk. A few things happen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548720,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right after merger that could lead to a bright\nburst of light to accompany the gravitational waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555240,
    "dDurationMs": 6039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These captured black hole binaries will be\nsurrounded by their own mini-vortices of gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561279,
    "dDurationMs": 4951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When they finally merge, they release a burst\nof gravitational radiation so powerful that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566230,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it can carry away up to several percent of\nthe original mass of the two black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571320,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gas that was orbiting the binary suddenly\nfinds itself moving too quickly for the reduced"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576340,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational field of the final black hole.\nIt creates an expanding expanding shock-front"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582660,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that then collides with the gas of the surrounding\naccretion disk. Some of the shocked gas will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587800,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then fall back in, resulting in a burst of\naccretion into the new, merged black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593530,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally, the release of gravitational waves\ndelivers a kick to the final black hole - a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598560,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bit like the recoil of a gun. This drives\nthe black hole and its surrounding gas through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603660,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the accretion disk, causing more shocks as\ngas is rammed together. All of this violent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609120,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "motion produces a flash of ultraviolet radiation\n- and those flashes may be visible to telescopes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614730,
    "dDurationMs": 3790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on Earth right after the gravitational waves\narrive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618520,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now it’s going to be a challenge\nspotting these. The combined resolution of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623390,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the two LIGO and the VIRGO observatories locates\na gravitational wave source to a pretty large"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629180,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blob on the sky, which will typically contain\nhundreds of active galactic nuclei and many"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634590,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thousands of regular galaxies. Any of those\ncould be the source of the black hole merger."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640770,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But since LIGO started operation we now have\nadvanced follow-up systems in place. As soon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646100,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a candidate wave is detected, multiple\ntelescopes scan that region of the sky to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651420,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "search for electromagnetic signatures. If\nthe merger was inside an accretion disk then we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657520,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might see a temporary increase in the light\nfrom one of the active galaxies — a fading"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662930,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flash that is brightest at ultraviolet wavelengths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667660,
    "dDurationMs": 4710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The researchers are currently scouring the\nfollow-up observations of past black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672370,
    "dDurationMs": 5719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mergers for just such a signature, and will\nbe keeping a close eye on future mergers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678089,
    "dDurationMs": 5421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With LIGO now detected a black hole merger\nevent every week or so, there’s a good chance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683510,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this will be spotted — assuming accretion\ndisk mergers are really happening, and that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689050,
    "dDurationMs": 3230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all the assumptions of the model hold up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692280,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyway, like I said: gravitational wave astronomy\nwill reveal many cosmic mysteries and strange"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698310,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "phenomena. Now we have the amazing possibility\nof black holes merging and growing to enormous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704040,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "size while trapped within the blazing vortices\naround even vaster supermassive black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710420,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Awesome, sure, but what else would you expect\nfrom this supremely badass and frankly, totally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716880,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "metal Space Time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719040,
    "dDurationMs": 3739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we're a little behind in comment responses\ndue to the holidays - in fact, due to this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722779,
    "dDurationMs": 4221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one - coming to you from the hot Australian\nsummer. We're going to catch up on comments"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727000,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over the next couple of episodes, but today we're\ndoing comments from our episode on cosmological"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731100,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "natural selection - Lee Smolin's idea that\nmaybe new universes are born inside black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737180,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A number of you made a similar observation/question: Shouldn't a universe that's born inside a black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742500,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be limited in mass by the amount of stuff\nthat falls into that black hole? Well, the answer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746180,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is ... not necessarily. There's at least one\nmechanism in which you can make an arbitrarily gigantic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750720,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe from very little matter and that is inflation, in which the rapid exponential expansion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756390,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of space can create a ridiculously large universe\nthat multiplies the density of a high-energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762040,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum field powering inflation. Check out\nour episodes on cosmic inflation to expand"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768640,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on that answer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769640,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A couple of you point out that the idea of\nblack holes birthing universes still doesn't"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773540,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explain where the first something-from-nothing actually\ncame from. That's true, but I don't think"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779200,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it' fair to cite this as a weakness of the\nidea. The hypothesis is trying to explain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783240,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the apparent fine tuning of the fundamental\nconstants, not the origin of everything. Ideas"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788640,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like cosmological natural selection and eternal\ninflation are helpful because they reduce the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793390,
    "dDurationMs": 6319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amount of work required by that initial creationary\nevent - it only needs to produce a spacetime"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799709,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "capable of exponential growth - after that\nthe fundamental constants sort of take care of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805149,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "themselves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806149,
    "dDurationMs": 3941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Xzayler asks what happens when a black hole\nis absorbed by another black hole? Do the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810090,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universes collide? Do the constants average\nout? The answer to this is ... no one has"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815390,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any idea. The cosmological natural selection\nhypothesis doesn't really define the ongoing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821050,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "connection between the initial black hole\nand the universe it spawns. That new universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825230,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is birthed in the formation of the black hole\nsingularity. If it grows by some inflation-like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830670,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansion into an entirely new spacetime then\nit may not care about the later evolution"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836040,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of its parent black hole - perhaps unaffected\nby whether it grows or merges or evaporates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842070,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Really kids these days, no respect at all. On that note New Message feels a bit better learning that even whole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848970,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universes can be a disappointment to their\nparents."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853320,
    "dDurationMs": 3170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many people also commented that they'd thought\nof the whole black holes creating new universes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856490,
    "dDurationMs": 6849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing independently to Lee Smolin. I'm making\na list, guys. If it turns out the whole cosmological natural selection"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863339,
    "dDurationMs": 5321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing is true, I'll send that list to the\nSwedish Academy of Sciences. I can see it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868660,
    "dDurationMs": 4369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now\" \"This year's nobel prize in physics is\nsplit between aclaimed theoretical physicist"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873029,
    "dDurationMs": 5251,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lee Smolin, xxfishytomatoxx, and 57 others from the internet who totally thought"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878280,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of it years ago dude.\""
    } ]
  } ]
}
